            "review_title": "Review at MyDeli - Homemade Pasta & Pizza",
            "review_text": "foodfighter23\nHanoi, Vietnam51 contributions\n0\nMy, my MyDeli\nAug 2018 • Family\nWe went to MyDeli to experience firsthand the 5 star facebook rating of this italian restaurant. It was our 10th year anniversary and we want to try something new so I sent them a FB message to reserve a table 7pm to make sure that we’d have a space in case the restaurant goes busy.\n\nLocation: MyDeli is quite far from the center of Hoan Kiem, and it will take you around 10-15 minute walk from the Hoan kiem Lake. Although, it is near one of the most prestigious hotel in Hanoi, the Melia hotel.\n\nArrival: We arrived earlier than expected and I told the cashier that I have a reservation (which Im not sure if she understood). But we were ushered by a man (i think he’s the owner) to the table by the wall.\n\nRestaurant interior: Btw, the restaurant is really very small. We were three, with me and my son being quite bigger than my husband. So we got this little square table by the wall, so with another table just a few inches from hours, we were sitting so close together without enough room to move comfortably. With the table’s size, it wouldn’t be enough to accommodate if the visitors would order lots of food. \n\nThe design is bright and cozy, very enticing and pleasing to the eyes.\n\nFood: Watching Masterchef almost always and having to Be so interested in tasting a Ravioli for so long, I ordered the spinach ricotta Ravioli and pumpkin soup. My husband ordered a salad, ham and cheese sandwich and mydeli pizza while we got chicken fingers for my son. We also ordered lime and calamansi juice respectively plus creme brulee for dessert.\n\nThe Spinach Ricotta Ravioli was okay, not that outstanding as I expect it to be and I didn’t taste the Ricotta cheese at all. I also feel that it may not be made fresh, as the filling is too thick and settled, as compared to a freshly made ravioli filling which is suppose to ooze out when you cut it in the center. The pasta is also kind of tough and slightly thick as opposed to a silky smooth and thin pasta when freshly made. The sauce was good though. \n\nSalad was good and the ham and cheese sandwich was just okay too although the sandwich despite its name for some reason contained a sunny side up egg. The highlight of the evening is the PIZZA! \n\nThe pizza was sooooooo gooood. It has a thin, crispy crust, full of the ingredients as stated on the menu and it was delicious. We enjoyed it very much alongside with the chicken fingers.\n\nPumpkin soup was served late, despite the fact that it was the first thing i ordered. Soup and salad should always be served first as they are the “light” meals aka appetizers which are supposed to get our tummies ready for the main dish. Unfortunately, it was served late, I think with the pizza or even later. Pumpkin soup tastes very good though.\n\nOn to dessert, the creme brulee. Phycially, It lived up to the expectations of a crusty top, that is supposed to crack when hit my the spoon. It did just like that. However, the creme brulee is not as creamy (when its supposed to be made up of heavy creme and egg yolks, so i was expecting a texture and taste similar to the pinoy leche flan) as I expect it to be. Its also not as sweet as I dreamed of. So we were kind of disappointed.\n\nPrice: Definitely inexpensive. We order quite a lot and we only billed around 500k VND and we even have a takeaway.\n\nService: the one who took my ordered seems to be quite inattentive as she forgot to include the souo which is the first one i ordered. Also, we noticed that while we eat, they seem to be watching our reactions so its kind of awkward. But, otherwise the staff is friendly and accommodating.\n\nOverall: You get your money’s worth. Not that impressive as the reviews, but will still give it another chance. I still want to check other pasta and pizza variety to see which ones are good.\nRead more\nWritten September 12, 2018",
